    "case_id": "36",
    "case_summary": "A 62-year-old woman with multiple sclerosis treated with fingolimod for 3 years presented with a tender nodule on her forehead, which had gradually grown over 3 weeks. She reported bumping her forehead on an air-conditioning unit several months prior. She denied fever, neck stiffness, and photophobia. She lived alone with a pet cat and spent minimal time outdoors. She had no recent exposure to systemic steroids.\n\n- Neurological examination was at baseline- Eroded, crusted papule on the left forehead- No other lesions on full-body skin examination\n\n{'Laboratory Tests': '- White blood cell count: 3.9/μL (normal 4000-1100/μL)- Lymphocyte count: 0.65/μL (normal 1000-5000/μL)- Absolute CD4 count: 56/μL (normal 560-1840/μL)- CD8 count: 121/μL (normal 260-1230/μL)- HIV test: Negative- Serum cryptococcal antigen: Negative- Blood cultures: Negative- Cerebrospinal fluid cryptococcal antigen: Negative- Cerebrospinal fluid cultures: Negative', 'Imaging Studies': '- Chest radiography: Negative', 'Pathology': '- Skin biopsy: Granulomatous inflammation composed of histiocytes, giant cells, and lymphocytes admixed with numerous narrow-budding yeasts with thick capsules- Tissue culture: Grew Cryptococcus neoformansImage Title: Cutaneous Cryptococcus Clinical and Histologic PhotographsImage Description: A) Eroded, crusted papule on the left forehead. B) Skin biopsy specimen showing granulomatous lymphohistiocytic inflammation surrounding thickly encapsulated yeasts'}\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Cutaneous cryptococcosis\nB. Primary cutaneous cryptococcosis (PCC)\nC. Cutaneous cryptococcal infection with systemic dissemination\nD. Deep Fungal Infection (e.g., Sporotrichosis)\n",
